WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:01.666 --> 00:00:05.050
We surveyed developers a few months ago,
asking which tasks

2
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.050
they thought agents were best suited
to perform today.

3
00:00:09.133 --> 00:00:12.434
The second most answered
one was personal assistants

4
00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:15.617
and productivity tasks.

5
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:19.067
One core aspect of these tasks

6
00:00:19.134 --> 00:00:22.134
is memory, specifically, long-term memory.

7
00:00:22.584 --> 00:00:24.801
Think about working with a human assistant

8
00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:27.801
who is serving
as a personal assistant of sorts.

9
00:00:28.635 --> 00:00:32.118
If they forgot all their conversations
with you and didn't remember

10
00:00:32.452 --> 00:00:36.885
things that you told them previously,
that would be a pretty bad experience.

11
00:00:37.035 --> 00:00:39.952
And the same goes for agents.

12
00:00:39.952 --> 00:00:44.186
So, memory is a key
part of these types of tasks.

13
00:00:44.452 --> 00:00:49.452
And so when we think about an example
through which to introduce memory,

14
00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:53.386
a task of this nature is a great fit.

15
00:00:56.153 --> 00:00:59.320
One example of a task
like this is an email agent.

16
00:01:00.970 --> 00:01:01.820
So email

17
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.870
is something that we all have
and we all have to deal with.

18
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.470
And as you get busier and busier,
you may have more and more emails

19
00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:11.954
popping up that you just don't have time
to look at or respond to.

20
00:01:11.954 --> 00:01:14.954
And this is perfect for an agent
to come in.

21
00:01:16.154 --> 00:01:19.604
If we think about what an agent
would need to do this job well.

22
00:01:19.904 --> 00:01:24.355
It's probably pretty similar
to what an executive assistant might need

23
00:01:24.371 --> 00:01:27.072
to do this job well.
They might need a calendar

24
00:01:27.072 --> 00:01:29.655
to check your calendar
to see where you're free.

25
00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:30.805
And they might need access

26
00:01:30.805 --> 00:01:34.705
to writing emails on your behalf
or in response to other emails.

27
00:01:35.305 --> 00:01:38.122
And so when we think about building
an agent, we can think about

28
00:01:38.122 --> 00:01:41.156
giving them access
to these types of tools.

29
00:01:43.823 --> 00:01:45.173
And an agent like this has a

30
00:01:45.173 --> 00:01:48.323
lot of places
where memory becomes really important.

31
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.473
First step is looking at all

32
00:01:51.473 --> 00:01:54.790
the emails and deciding,
should I ignore this?

33
00:01:54.790 --> 00:01:57.023
Should I respond to this?

34
00:01:57.023 --> 00:02:00.490
If it does decide to respond to it
and decides to use the calendar

35
00:02:00.524 --> 00:02:05.024
email tool, it needs to know
what is the person's meeting preferences,

36
00:02:05.541 --> 00:02:08.541
both in terms of time,
but also location and title.

37
00:02:09.191 --> 00:02:10.491
And then if it decides to write an email,

38
00:02:10.491 --> 00:02:12.291
it needs to know
what is this person's tone?

39
00:02:12.291 --> 00:02:14.425
What is their style?

40
00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:18.591
And then there might also be interactions
with people that they've had previously.

41
00:02:18.925 --> 00:02:21.925
This is important
context to be able to know, to respond to.

42
00:02:24.475 --> 00:02:25.825
And so this email assistant

43
00:02:25.825 --> 00:02:28.825
is a great place to show off

44
00:02:28.825 --> 00:02:31.842
what LLMs and memory can do together.

45
00:02:32.809 --> 00:02:35.442
And so we're going to use
it as a proofing ground for talking about

46
00:02:35.442 --> 00:02:38.726
three different types of memory
for AI agents.

47
00:02:39.793 --> 00:02:42.793
So, first the semantic memory.

48
00:02:43.376 --> 00:02:45.410
So, what is semantic memory?

49
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:47.327
It's basically facts.

50
00:02:47.327 --> 00:02:49.310
If we think about semantic memory
in humans,

51
00:02:49.310 --> 00:02:52.194
it's things that you've learned, facts
that you've learned in school.

52
00:02:52.194 --> 00:02:55.194
By reading a textbook that you remember
from previous interactions,

53
00:02:55.194 --> 00:02:58.194
all these kind of like facts
based type of memory.

54
00:02:58.994 --> 00:03:01.727
A second type of memory is episodic
memory.

55
00:03:01.727 --> 00:03:03.011
This is experiences.

56
00:03:03.011 --> 00:03:06.861
This is callbacks to exact conversations
that you've had.

57
00:03:08.478 --> 00:03:11.295
It's not necessarily facts
about these conversations.

58
00:03:11.295 --> 00:03:13.545
It's just the memory of this conversation
itself.

59
00:03:13.545 --> 00:03:16.545
The memory of going to Disneyland
or something like that.

60
00:03:17.862 --> 00:03:20.062
And the third
type of memory is procedural memory.

61
00:03:21.612 --> 00:03:24.312
These are kind of like instructions
for yourself

62
00:03:24.312 --> 00:03:27.262
or instincts or motor skills,
how to ride a bike,

63
00:03:27.262 --> 00:03:31.096
or instructions that you give yourself
for how to respond to emails,

64
00:03:31.096 --> 00:03:33.646
for example.

65
00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:35.996
How do we map these onto agents?

66
00:03:35.996 --> 00:03:39.763
So, for semantic memory,
these might be facts about a user

67
00:03:39.763 --> 00:03:43.163
or about people placed things
that the agent has interacted with.

68
00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:47.930
For episodic,
this might be past agent actions.

69
00:03:48.264 --> 00:03:51.047
So in the form of few shot examples.

70
00:03:51.047 --> 00:03:55.014
So actual literal kind of like trajectory
of what have happened before.

71
00:03:55.964 --> 00:03:58.098
And then from procedural
this is maybe the most straightforward.

72
00:03:58.098 --> 00:03:59.781
This is a system prompt.

73
00:03:59.781 --> 00:04:02.781
The instructions
that you give the agent on how to behave.

74
00:04:04.965 --> 00:04:08.715
Besides these three types of memories,
it's also worth calling out

75
00:04:08.715 --> 00:04:12.132
the different ways that these agents
can interact with these memories.

76
00:04:13.615 --> 00:04:16.015
So there's two different paradigms
that we see.

77
00:04:16.015 --> 00:04:18.199
One is generally in the hot path.

78
00:04:18.199 --> 00:04:23.116
So this is where the agent updates
memories as it's responding to the user.

79
00:04:23.116 --> 00:04:25.933
So it's all in one go.

80
00:04:25.933 --> 00:04:28.933
A separate type is when the updates
of the memory

81
00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:32.033
happen in the background
or in a separate process.

82
00:04:33.166 --> 00:04:35.267
There's pros and cons to each of these.

83
00:04:35.267 --> 00:04:38.933
When you update things in the hot path,
you generally just have one agent.

84
00:04:39.383 --> 00:04:40.967
And so that's simpler to maintain.

85
00:04:40.967 --> 00:04:43.967
And the updates often happen
instantaneously.

86
00:04:45.201 --> 00:04:48.834
However, the downside
is that you now have this agent

87
00:04:48.834 --> 00:04:50.034
that has to do both these things.

88
00:04:50.034 --> 00:04:52.434
It has to update memories
and response to users.

89
00:04:52.434 --> 00:04:54.134
So it's more complicated.

90
00:04:54.134 --> 00:04:57.618
And it also adds an additional latency
when responding to the user.

91
00:04:59.185 --> 00:05:01.485
In the background has the flip.

92
00:05:01.485 --> 00:05:04.002
So now you can separate the updated
memory

93
00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:07.002
from the agent's core

94
00:05:07.152 --> 00:05:08.452
things that it needs to do.

95
00:05:08.452 --> 00:05:10.635
So it's simpler.

96
00:05:10.635 --> 00:05:13.636
And you remove the updating
from the hot path so it actually

97
00:05:13.786 --> 00:05:16.036
responds faster.

98
00:05:16.036 --> 00:05:18.736
The issue is that
now you have two agents instead of one.

99
00:05:18.736 --> 00:05:20.603
So that's more complicated.

100
00:05:20.603 --> 00:05:23.353
And the updates might not happen
instantaneously.

101
00:05:23.353 --> 00:05:26.353
They might only happen
when the updating memory gets kicked off.

102
00:05:29.603 --> 00:05:30.953
So in this course

103
00:05:30.953 --> 00:05:34.187
we are going to apply these memory types
to an email agent.

104
00:05:35.087 --> 00:05:37.254
So we're going to start
with a basic email agent

105
00:05:37.254 --> 00:05:40.254
that first triages the incoming emails.

106
00:05:40.404 --> 00:05:43.421
And if it's worth responding to it
passes it on to a second agent,

107
00:05:43.721 --> 00:05:47.454
which uses a calendar tool and a writing
tool to respond to the email.

108
00:05:49.855 --> 00:05:50.621
The first type of

109
00:05:50.621 --> 00:05:53.621
memory that we're going to add
in is semantic memory.

110
00:05:54.471 --> 00:05:56.688
And so we're going to do this
by giving this agent that's

111
00:05:56.688 --> 00:05:59.688
responding to emails more tools.

112
00:05:59.688 --> 00:06:02.722
Tools for writing memories

113
00:06:02.922 --> 00:06:05.922
and tools for reading memories.

114
00:06:07.306 --> 00:06:10.306
The agent's
going to use these tools in the hot path.

115
00:06:10.639 --> 00:06:13.706
So as it's looping
and as it's calling the calendar

116
00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:18.323
or writing the email, it's going to be
writing memories and retrieving memories.

117
00:06:19.373 --> 00:06:22.090
So this is semantic memory
in the hot path.

118
00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.090
And we're doing this
by giving the agent a tool.

119
00:06:26.657 --> 00:06:29.024
Then we're going to add episodic memory.

120
00:06:29.024 --> 00:06:31.490
So this is in the form of few short
examples

121
00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:34.191
that are going to be in the triage step.

122
00:06:34.191 --> 00:06:38.924
So here we're going to have a few shot
examples that correspond to emails.

123
00:06:38.924 --> 00:06:42.291
And then it's also going to have
what we want the triage result to be.

124
00:06:42.291 --> 00:06:45.825
So whether it should be ignored
or notified or responded to.

125
00:06:47.141 --> 00:06:47.591
And so these are

126
00:06:47.591 --> 00:06:50.592
going to be inserted into the prompt right
here.

127
00:06:51.792 --> 00:06:54.792
We're going to add these examples
in a separate process.

128
00:06:55.242 --> 00:06:58.242
So this is an example of episodic memory

129
00:06:58.725 --> 00:07:01.726
where the updates happen
in the background.

130
00:07:02.992 --> 00:07:05.993
And finally, we're going to add
in procedural memory.

131
00:07:06.476 --> 00:07:09.893
So these are the prompts themselves
or rather parts of the prompts

132
00:07:10.193 --> 00:07:14.693
with instructions for how to use
different tools or how to triage things.

133
00:07:15.793 --> 00:07:18.793
We are going to update this
in the background as well.

134
00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:22.510
And we're actually going to use a separate
agent

135
00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.510
to do the updates.

136
00:07:25.577 --> 00:07:28.511
Although we're working
with a very specific email agent,

137
00:07:28.511 --> 00:07:31.511
which we're going to build in lesson two.

138
00:07:32.844 --> 00:07:36.494
The techniques for managing agent memory
that you learn here

139
00:07:36.861 --> 00:07:41.095
will be applicable to any agents
that you build in the future.

140
00:07:43.812 --> 00:07:44.545
You'll want to think

141
00:07:44.545 --> 00:07:48.345
carefully about what types of memories
your agent needs.

142
00:07:48.762 --> 00:07:51.045
Does it need to learn better instructions?

143
00:07:51.045 --> 00:07:53.329
Okay, maybe that's procedural.

144
00:07:53.329 --> 00:07:55.812
Do you want to give it a few shot examples
because you think that will help

145
00:07:55.812 --> 00:07:56.896
really guide its actions?

146
00:07:56.896 --> 00:07:59.396
Okay. That's episodic.

147
00:07:59.396 --> 00:08:02.329
Does it just need to know facts
about people place and things?

148
00:08:02.329 --> 00:08:04.013
Okay. That's semantic.

149
00:08:04.013 --> 00:08:07.013
It all kind of depends on the application
that you're building.

150
00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.847
With that, let's jump to the next lesson

151
00:08:10.847 --> 00:08:14.180
and learn
how to build this baseline email agent.

